<!-- Dedicated baseline for the async insert queue route of INSERT ... SELECT (see
     InterpreterInsertQuery::addInsertToSelectPipeline and AsyncInsertQueueTransform), so a
     regression in that specific route is not masked by every other insert test, which does not
     set async_insert explicitly and mostly exercises the bulk, pass-through shape instead.

     The second query below is a comparator with the same insert shape but async_insert turned
     off, so it takes the ordinary, non queue route. The two queries differ only in the
     async_insert and wait_for_async_insert settings; everything else, the table, the SELECT
     shape and the row count, is identical, so a regression that is specific to the queue route
     is visible as a change in the first query relative to the second, rather than being hidden
     by a shift that affects both routes equally.

     The queue route is expected to be slower here by design, by at least
     async_insert_busy_timeout_min_ms, because this INSERT ... SELECT is small; that gap is
     accepted and is not itself a regression. -->
<test>
    <create_query>CREATE TABLE insert_select_async_insert_queue (n UInt64) ENGINE = MergeTree ORDER BY n</create_query>

    <query>INSERT INTO insert_select_async_insert_queue SELECT number FROM numbers(100) SETTINGS async_insert = 1, wait_for_async_insert = 1</query>
    <query>INSERT INTO insert_select_async_insert_queue SELECT number FROM numbers(100) SETTINGS async_insert = 0</query>

    <drop_query>DROP TABLE IF EXISTS insert_select_async_insert_queue</drop_query>
</test>
